okay so we have to spend some time today  talking about the general election in  the United Kingdom late last week  because there is so much being said  about it and how it does or doesn't  relate to the upcoming 2020 election  here in the United States and Donald  Trump and Bernie Sanders and there's a  lot of interesting and good thoughtful  takes about this but there's also some  bad talking points and misinformation  that's being spread so let's start with  the facts what happened in the UK  trumpian  Conservative Party Prime Minister Boris  Johnson won with the Tories the  right-wing party getting a majority of  80 seats in the House of Commons  this truly is huge and it's especially  huge because the Tories had just lost  the majority back in 2017 and then  meanwhile a crushing defeat for the  Labour Party and its prime minister  candidate then leader who has since  resigned Jeremy Corbyn major losses  ended up with the lowest proportion of  seats since 1935 did the Labour Party  but a brutal defeat I mean that's the  only way I can really characterize it  and Jeremy Corbyn has resigned as the  head of the Labour Party now meanwhile  the Scottish National Party won a huge  victory in Scotland winning 48 of 59  seats that has some interesting  relationships to the Scottish  independence movement the brexit and the  view of brexit in Scotland and there's a  lot to discuss there as well so let's go  through it in pieces was this a  landslide for the right in the United  Kingdom it was absolutely is it a  crushing defeat for labour considering  that it is sort of the closest to the  modern American progressive movement of  Bernie Sanders and Elizabeth Warren it  is absolutely a crushing defeat for  labour in the UK but this is where a lot  of the analyses started to start to go  off the rails because when we think  further there are people saying this is  a signal what happened in the UK is a  signal that there is a rejection of the  Bernie Warren ideology and policy  prescriptions happening worldwide and it  signals that someone with Bernie or  Warren's platforms here in the United  States will get crushed in 2020 Trump is  going to win  the Democrats would be very very wrong  to go with someone like Bernie Sanders  or Elizabeth Warren because they can't  win as proven by the defeat of labour in  the UK this analysis is too simplistic  so let's try to flesh it out a little  bit and look at it more closely first of  all the starting point in the UK is a  much stronger social democracy then we  have here in the United States when you  look at the institutions that they  already have for example the UK already  has nationalized health care the NHS now  I know that there are some ideas of  Boris Johnson dismantling it but the  point is they've had it for a long time  it is popular voting for labour is not  how you might get such a system of  nationalized health care they have it  similarly on education in the UK on  other safety nets in so many other areas  these policies are already part of  British society so when you realize that  they already have these things labor  there is not like Bernie or Warren here  because here the left is trying to get  lots of things for the first time that  the UK already has so the stakes are  very different and therefore comparing  what happened to labour in the UK to a  Bernie Warren candidacy or presidency or  nomination on the Democratic side here  is not an apt comparison in that way in  the United States the people offering a  lot of those programs that are already  part of British society are the left  most of those running in the Democratic  Party whereas in the UK many of these  programs have been in place for decades  so labour being brutally defeated in the  UK which they were doesn't confirm that  the policy of Bernie Warren Social  Democrats is doomed to fail in the  United States now that gets us to labour  as a specific group of people Jeremy  Corbyn as a person versus Bernie Warren  etc having a candidate who was dogged by  allegations of coddling anti-semitism  not taking it seriously having unsavory  characters in their coalition and who  was seen as flaccid in his  sponses was not good now we don't need  to argue about each claim related to  anti-semitism because I know there are  people who say no look he did this  investigation into this anti-semitic  allegation we don't need to argue about  it I don't want to make this an  adjudication of each claim you can  research it for yourself without  adjudicating each claim I think we would  all agree that the allegations clouded  labor for a long time and they were not  definitively done away with in the eyes  of the voters and then further by the  way right after this election former  London Mayor Ken Livingstone who's a  former Labour Party MP who was actually  suspended over anti-semitism came out  and said I told you labour lost because  of the unhelpful Jewish vote which is  not only insane it's also mathematically  impossible there are roughly 400,000  Jews in the UK that includes children  that includes you know that that's  everybody and Corbyn lost the popular  vote by nearly 4 million votes so I  don't even know why I have to mention  that but obviously those types of claims  are absurd but they're exactly the type  of stuff that people are concerned about  labour had a cloud over it that is  specific to labour that being said I  have pointed out that Bernie's got some  problematic surrogates Linda Sarsour is  one also dogged with anti-semitism in  her past I think Bernie should separate  from her I just don't know that that is  going to take Bernie down this narrative  has not become a predominant one in the  United States Elizabeth Warren had a  racist homophobic trans black woman as a  surrogate we called her out I believe  she has been removed from the warran  campaign but what's not being mentioned  in all of this is brexit which is  looming over everything in the UK in a  way that it is not here in the United  States so in addition to the stakes  being different because of the social  programs that the UK already has in  addition to the cloud over Corbin being  very different than anything affecting  Bernie or Warren right now Labour's  position on brexit amounted to maybe  okay which is a very weak position and  probably didn't help them so what can we  glean from the UK results we can't say  Bernie Warren type politicians are dead  in the water because they're not we  can't say Americans don't want  progressive policy on health care and  education and corporate reform because  by  large the polls tell us that they do  what we do need to say is what I've been  saying for a year no matter what the  hypothetical matchups might say Trump  might absolutely crush everyone and we  need to fight for every single vote no  constituency can be assumed to be with  us until they go out and vote in 2020 we  can confirm that having a policy and a  set of ideas is vastly more important  than a grievance candidate or an anti  Trump candidacy that I think is a lesson  it was a right-wing landslide in the UK  but it's a cautionary tale about the  2020 election not it's time to throw in  the towel because Bernie Warren types  are obviously being rejected different  situation different electorate different  status quo and starting point and the  answer to it all is the same thing that  we knew before the UK election which is  get out the vote assume nothing and we  will actually have some interesting  polling later that confirms that all is  not well so to speak  but more on that coming up a little bit  later today's program brought to you in  part by Blink accom / Pakman blinka is a  great service that I use all the time  what they do is they take thousands of  the most popular non-fiction books  including the newest bestsellers they  condense them into 15-minute audiobooks  that you can listen to and you still get  the most crucial information and  insi